<DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
    <div>
        With a custom item type, the NameSelectors tell the timeline which member of that type carries each
        value, so any existing model can feed the timeline without being reshaped.
    </div>
    <br />
    <BitTimeline Items="basicCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Horizontal" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        The Timeline offers two orientations: vertical (default) and horizontal. In the horizontal one the
        line runs from the start to the end of the timeline and the contents of each item stack above and
        below their dot.
    </div>
    <br />
    <BitTimeline Horizontal Items="basicCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Disabled" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        Disabling the whole timeline with IsEnabled dims every item and stops all of them from responding,
        while the IsEnabled selector of a single item only takes that one out of the interaction.
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Disabled component</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Horizontal Items="basicCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" IsEnabled="false" />
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Disabled item</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Horizontal Items="disabledCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Variant" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        The variant determines how the dots are painted: Fill (default) is a solid dot, Outline is a hollow
        dot with a colored border, and Text drops the dot's shape and keeps only its icon.
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Fill</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Horizontal Variant="BitVariant.Fill" Items="disabledCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Outline</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Horizontal Variant="BitVariant.Outline" Items="disabledCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Text</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Horizontal Variant="BitVariant.Text" Items="disabledCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Icon" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        Each item can render an icon inside its dot through the IconName selector, which turns the dot into
        a marker of what the event actually was.
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Fill</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Horizontal Items="iconCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" Variant="BitVariant.Fill" />
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Outline</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Horizontal Items="iconCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" Variant="BitVariant.Outline" />
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Text</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Horizontal Items="iconCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" Variant="BitVariant.Text" />
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Reversed" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        Reversed swaps the two sides of the line: on the timeline it flips every item at once, and through
        the Reversed selector it moves only a single item to the opposite side.
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Vertical</div><br />
    <div class="example-content">
        <BitTimeline Items="basicCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" Reversed />
        <BitTimeline Items="reversedCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Horizontal</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Horizontal Items="basicCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" Reversed />
    <br /><br />
    <BitTimeline Horizontal Items="reversedCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Alternate" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        Alternate puts every second item on the opposite side of the line, so the items zig-zag around it.
        Combined with Reversed the zig-zag starts from the other side.
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Vertical</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Alternate Items="twoSidedCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Vertical & Reversed</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Alternate Reversed Items="twoSidedCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Horizontal</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Alternate Horizontal Items="twoSidedCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Reverse order" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example8CsharpCode" Id="example8" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        ReverseOrder renders the list from its last item to its first one, which turns a chronological list
        into a most-recent-first one without touching the data itself.
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Vertical</div><br />
    <BitTimeline ReverseOrder Items="twoSidedCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Horizontal</div><br />
    <BitTimeline ReverseOrder Horizontal Items="twoSidedCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Truncate line" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example9CsharpCode" Id="example9" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        By default the connecting line runs through the whole extent of the timeline, past the first and the
        last dot. TruncateLine cuts it back to the first dot (Start), to the last one (End), or to both (Both),
        which is what a self-contained timeline usually wants.
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Both</div><br />
    <BitTimeline TruncateLine="BitTimelineTruncateLine.Both" Items="basicCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Start</div><br />
    <BitTimeline TruncateLine="BitTimelineTruncateLine.Start" Items="basicCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>End</div><br />
    <BitTimeline TruncateLine="BitTimelineTruncateLine.End" Items="basicCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Horizontal (Both)</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Horizontal TruncateLine="BitTimelineTruncateLine.Both" Items="basicCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Line variant" RazorCode="@example10RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example10CsharpCode" Id="example10" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        LineVariant paints the connecting line solid (default), dashed or dotted, and the LineVariant selector
        overrides it for a single item's stretch of the line. That is the usual way to separate the part of a
        timeline that has already happened from the part that is still expected.
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Dashed</div><br />
    <BitTimeline LineVariant="BitTimelineLineVariant.Dashed" Items="basicCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Dotted</div><br />
    <BitTimeline LineVariant="BitTimelineLineVariant.Dotted" Items="basicCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Item's LineVariant</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Items="lineVariantCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" TruncateLine="BitTimelineTruncateLine.Both" />
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Horizontal (Dashed)</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Horizontal LineVariant="BitTimelineLineVariant.Dashed" Items="basicCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Item customization" RazorCode="@example11RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example11CsharpCode" Id="example11" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        The Color, Size and Variant selectors override the values of the timeline for a single item, and the
        HideDot selector drops the dot of an item while keeping its contents in place.
    </div>
    <br />
    <BitTimeline Items="customizedCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Templates" RazorCode="@example12RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example12CsharpCode" Id="example12" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        The PrimaryContent, SecondaryContent and DotTemplate selectors replace the corresponding part of an
        item, while the Template selector replaces the whole item, dot and line included. The timeline also
        accepts an ItemTemplate and a DotTemplate of its own, which are used by every item that brings no
        template of its own.
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Vertical</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Items="templateItems" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Horizontal</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Horizontal Items="templateItems" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" />
    <br /><br /><br />
    <div>Item's Template & the timeline's DotTemplate</div><br />
    <BitTimeline Items="fullTemplateItems" NameSelectors="nameSelectors" TruncateLine="BitTimelineTruncateLine.Both">
        <DotTemplate Context="item">
            <div class="dot-template"><BitIcon IconName="@BitIconName.CheckMark" /></div>
        </DotTemplate>
    </BitTimeline>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Events" RazorCode="@example13RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example13CsharpCode" Id="example13" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        The OnItemClick of the timeline is raised for every item, and the OnClick selector points at a
        handler carried by the item itself; both of them run for the same click. Only the items that
        actually listen to a click become focusable buttons, and a disabled item raises neither of them.
        A clickable item picks up a hover plate and a focus ring, and answers to Enter and Space as well as to a click.
    </div>
    <br />
    <BitTimeline Items="clickCustoms" NameSelectors="nameSelectors"
                 OnItemClick="@((Event item) => { clickedCustom = $"{item.FirstText} (OnItemClick)"; })" />
    <br />
    <div>Clicked item: <b>@clickedCustom</b></div>
</DemoExample>

